p.src:
Let $\ket{r}$ and $\ket{l}$ denote the states with definite $x$-spin: $\ket{r}\longleftrightarrow\sigma_x=+1,\qquad
\ket{l}\longleftrightarrow\sigma_x=-1.$ Because the space is two-dimensional, each must be a linear combination of $\ket{u}$ and $\ket{d}$. A right-prepared spin gives up and down with equal probability, so the two coefficients must have equal magnitude. Choose the simplest phase convention: $\ket{r}=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\bigl(\ket{u}+\ket{d}\bigr).$ Right and left are distinguishable with certainty by the $x$-apparatus, so the left state must be orthogonal to the right state. One convenient choice is $\ket{l}=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\bigl(\ket{u}-\ket{d}\bigr).$ Indeed, $\begin{aligned}
\braket{l}{r}
&=\frac12(\bra{u}-\bra{d})(\ket{u}+\ket{d})\\
&=\frac12(1-1)=0.\end{aligned}$

